---
import MqttTopicLayout from '@components/MqttLearn/MqttTopicLayout.astro';
import MqttDiagram from '@components/MqttLearn/MqttDiagram.astro';
import LearnIconRows from '@components/MqttLearn/LearnIconRows.astro';
import LearnIconRow from '@components/MqttLearn/LearnIconRow.astro';
import LearnTypeCards from '@components/MqttLearn/LearnTypeCards.astro';
import type { FaqItem } from '@components/MqttLearn/FaqAccordion.astro';

const faq: FaqItem[] = [
	{
		q: 'Is an MQTT broker a server?',
		a: 'Yes. The broker is the <strong>central server</strong> in an MQTT deployment. Every client connects to it, and it is responsible for receiving published messages and forwarding them to the right subscribers.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What is the difference between an MQTT broker and an MQTT client?',
		a: 'A client is any device or application that publishes or subscribes; the broker is the <strong>single hub</strong> they all connect to. Clients never talk to each other directly — the broker routes every message between them.',
	},
	{
		q: 'Do I need a broker to use MQTT?',
		a: 'Yes. MQTT is a broker-centric publish/subscribe protocol, so <strong>a broker is required</strong>. There is no peer-to-peer mode; publishers and subscribers are decoupled through the broker.',
	},
	{
		q: 'What are examples of MQTT brokers?',
		a: 'There are many open-source and commercial brokers. <strong>TBMQ</strong> is an open-source MQTT broker built by ThingsBoard for very large-scale deployments.',
	},
	{
		q: 'How many devices can an MQTT broker handle?',
		a: 'It depends on the broker and its architecture. Lightweight single-node brokers handle thousands to hundreds of thousands of connections; clustered brokers like TBMQ are designed to scale to <strong>100M+ concurrent connections</strong>.',
	},
];
---

<MqttTopicLayout slug="mqtt-broker" faq={faq}>
	<p>
		If <a href="/mqtt/what-is-mqtt/">MQTT</a> is the language IoT devices speak, the broker is the switchboard they all call
		into. It is the one component every client connects to, and it is what makes MQTT's decoupled publish/subscribe model
		work.
	</p>

	<h2>What an MQTT broker does</h2>
	<p>
		Clients don't send messages to each other — they send them to <strong>topics</strong> on the broker. When a message is
		published, the broker finds every client subscribed to a matching topic and delivers a copy to each one. A single publish
		can fan out to many subscribers, and a subscriber can receive from many publishers, all without any of them knowing about
		the others.
	</p>

	<MqttDiagram caption="The broker decouples publishers from subscribers and fans each message out">
		<svg viewBox="0 0 680 264" role="img" aria-label="Broker routing one publish to many subscribers">
			<text class="d-tiny d-mid" x="95" y="20">PUBLISHERS</text><text class="d-tiny d-mid" x="590" y="20"
				>SUBSCRIBERS</text
			>
			<rect class="d-actor" x="30" y="40" width="130" height="36" rx="9"></rect><text
				class="d-alabel d-mid"
				x="95"
				y="63">Sensor A</text
			>
			<rect class="d-actor" x="30" y="103" width="130" height="36" rx="9"></rect><text
				class="d-alabel d-mid"
				x="95"
				y="126">Sensor B</text
			>
			<rect class="d-actor" x="30" y="166" width="130" height="36" rx="9"></rect><text
				class="d-alabel d-mid"
				x="95"
				y="189">App X</text
			>
			<rect class="d-actor d-actor--hero" x="255" y="58" width="170" height="126" rx="12"></rect><text
				class="d-alabel d-alabel--hero d-mid"
				x="340"
				y="84"
				style="font-size:14px;letter-spacing:.04em">BROKER</text
			>
			<text class="d-feat d-mid" x="340" y="110">match topic</text><text class="d-feat d-mid" x="340" y="129"
				>apply QoS</text
			><text class="d-feat d-mid" x="340" y="148">auth + ACLs</text><text class="d-feat d-mid" x="340" y="167"
				>manage session</text
			>
			<rect class="d-actor" x="520" y="40" width="140" height="36" rx="9"></rect><text
				class="d-alabel d-mid"
				x="590"
				y="63">Dashboard</text
			>
			<rect class="d-actor" x="520" y="103" width="140" height="36" rx="9"></rect><text
				class="d-alabel d-mid"
				x="590"
				y="126">Service</text
			>
			<rect class="d-actor" x="520" y="166" width="140" height="36" rx="9"></rect><text
				class="d-alabel d-mid"
				x="590"
				y="189">Logger</text
			>
			<path class="d-line" d="M160 58 C210 58 205 104 252 112" marker-end="url(#arGray)"></path><path
				class="d-line"
				d="M160 121 L252 121"
				marker-end="url(#arGray)"></path><path
				class="d-line"
				d="M160 184 C210 184 205 138 252 130"
				marker-end="url(#arGray)"></path><text class="d-tiny d-mid" x="182" y="105">publish</text>
			<path class="d-line d-line--green" d="M425 112 C472 104 470 58 517 58" marker-end="url(#arGreen)"></path><path
				class="d-line d-line--green"
				d="M425 121 L517 121"
				marker-end="url(#arGreen)"></path><path
				class="d-line d-line--green"
				d="M425 130 C472 138 470 184 517 184"
				marker-end="url(#arGreen)"></path><text class="d-tiny d-mid" x="483" y="105" style="fill:#4f7d63">route</text>
			<text class="d-note d-mid" x="340" y="222">one publish in → many deliveries out</text>
		</svg>
	</MqttDiagram>

	<h2>What the broker manages</h2>
	<p>Beyond routing, the broker owns the state and rules of the whole system:</p>
	<LearnIconRows>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:plug-connected" title="Connections">
			accepting clients over TCP, TLS, and <a href="/mqtt/websocket/">WebSocket</a>, and keeping them alive with <a
				href="/mqtt/keep-alive/">keep-alive</a
			>.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:sitemap" title="Subscriptions & topic matching">
			tracking who is subscribed to what, including <a href="/mqtt/topics/">wildcard</a> subscriptions.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:shield-check" title="Delivery guarantees">
			honoring each message's <a href="/mqtt/qos/">QoS</a> level.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:database" title="State">
			<a href="/mqtt/retained-messages/">retained messages</a>, <a href="/mqtt/last-will/">last will</a>, and <a
				href="/mqtt/persistent-session/">persistent sessions</a
			> for offline clients.
		</LearnIconRow>
		<LearnIconRow icon="tabler:lock" title="Security">
			<a href="/mqtt/security/">authenticating</a> clients and authorizing which topics each may use.
		</LearnIconRow>
	</LearnIconRows>

	<h2>Types of MQTT brokers</h2>
	<p>Brokers come in a few flavors, and the right one depends on how much you want to run yourself:</p>
	<LearnTypeCards
		cards={[
			{
				icon: 'tabler:brand-open-source',
				title: 'Open-source',
				text: 'Free and self-hosted — you deploy and scale it. Ideal for prototyping, full control, and integrating into your own stack.',
				highlight: true,
				badge: "You're here — TBMQ",
			},
			{
				icon: 'tabler:building-skyscraper',
				title: 'Commercial',
				text: 'Enterprise builds that add clustering, management dashboards, advanced security, and SLA-backed support.',
			},
			{
				icon: 'tabler:cloud',
				title: 'Cloud / MQTT-as-a-Service',
				text: 'Fully managed offerings where the provider handles deployment, scaling, and uptime, so you run no infrastructure yourself.',
			},
			{
				icon: 'tabler:cpu',
				title: 'Embedded',
				text: 'Lightweight brokers that run on a gateway or inside an application for local, offline-first processing at the edge.',
			},
		]}
	/>
	<p>
		TBMQ is open-source, with a professional edition and a managed private-cloud option — so you can start self-hosted
		and move to managed hosting later without switching brokers.
	</p>

	<h2>What to look for in a broker</h2>
	<p>
		Brokers differ most in how far they scale and how they persist data. For production IoT, the questions that matter
		are: how many concurrent connections and how much throughput it sustains, whether it clusters horizontally, which
		MQTT versions it supports (such as 3.1.1 and 5.0), how it persists messages for offline clients, and what
		authentication and authorization it offers. For the long run, also weigh the strength of its community or commercial
		support and the total cost of running it.
	</p>

	<h2>The broker in TBMQ</h2>
	<p>
		TBMQ is an open-source MQTT broker supporting MQTT 3.1, 3.1.1, and 5.0, engineered to scale to 100M+ concurrent
		connections on a single cluster by using Kafka as its internal backbone. The
		<a href="/docs/mqtt-broker/user-guide/mqtt-broker/">TBMQ broker guide</a> covers what sets it apart and how its DEVICE
		and APPLICATION clients differ. To see how the pieces fit together, the
		<a href="/docs/mqtt-broker/architecture/">architecture overview</a> walks through the design, and
		<a href="/docs/mqtt-broker/getting-started/">getting started</a> spins one up in minutes.
	</p>
</MqttTopicLayout>
